# A new kernel from the AI host (beehive), over the network link the machine
# already has: `update` fetches it, checks it and puts it on trial in the
# spare slot; the A/B rollback decides at the next boot.
@bootdisk
@net
@ai-net
$ net dhcp
~ 10\.0\.2\.15
$ update
~ no AI bridge
$ ai connect {hive} {hive-name} {hive-key}
connected (fake fake)
$ update
~ ^no newer kernel on offer \(this is b\d+\)$
$ update auto
automatic updates: off ('update auto on' allows them)

@offer newer
$ update
~ ^  fetching kernel b\d+ \(\d+ KiB\)$
~ ^  writing the new kernel \(b\d+\) into slot b$
  checking what was written
  putting slot b on trial
~ ^kernel b\d+ is in slot b, on trial at the next start: reboot$
$ update
~ ^kernel b\d+ is on offer, but another kernel is still on trial$
@reboot
~ ^\[slots\] trying slot b \(b\d+\): rollback armed
~ ^\[slots\] slot b passed its health check: it is now the good slot$
$ slots
booted slot: b
good slot:   b
$ net dhcp
$ ai
~ beehive online
$ update
~ ^kernel b\d+ is on offer, but it is installed already$

# The same kernel under its own build number, or no offer: nothing to do.
@offer
$ update
~ ^no newer kernel on offer
@offer none
$ update
~ ^no newer kernel on offer
$ update sideways
usage: update [auto [on | off]]
@signal "target": "test", "kind": "health", "text": "slot b b\d+ passed its health check"
